---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { zones, rules, aliases, networkEvidence } from "../../data/network";
const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const segments=zones.filter(z=>z.kind==="VLAN");
---
<BaseLayout title="Network" description="Follow Nova Corp's five VLANs, WireGuard tunnel, ordered firewall rules and protected company services." breadcrumbs={[{label:"Explore",href:"map/"},{label:"Network"}]}>
<section class="page-head"><div class="container">
 <div class="eyebrow">Network / Company traffic</div>
 <h1>Where can a company computer connect?</h1>
 <p class="lead" style="margin-top:18px">pfSense connects Nova Corp's separate network areas. The networks define where systems live; the ordered firewall rules decide which connections can pass. Some broad rules remain documented limitations.</p>
 <div class="button-row" style="margin-top:25px"><a class="btn" href={base+"network/zones/client/"}>Follow an employee request →</a><a class="btn ghost" href={base+"network/rules/"}>Read the firewall rules in order</a></div>
</div></section>
<section class="section compact"><div class="container">
 <div class="stats-strip" style="margin:0 0 36px"><div class="stat"><b>{segments.length}</b><span>Configured VLANs</span></div><div class="stat"><b>{rules.length}</b><span>Explicit saved firewall rules</span></div><div class="stat"><b>{aliases.length}</b><span>Reusable firewall aliases</span></div></div>
 <div class="section-heading"><div><div class="eyebrow">Select a network area</div><h2>Explore how the company is segmented</h2></div><a class="text-link" href={base+"network/rules/"}>All rules →</a></div>
 <div class="area-grid">{zones.map(z=><a class="area-card" href={base+"network/zones/"+z.slug+"/"} style={"--accent:"+(z.slug==="vpn_remote"?"#0F766E":z.slug==="client"?"#7C3AED":"#2563EB")}>
   <div class="number"><span>{z.kind}</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></div><h3>{z.name}</h3>
   <p>{z.purpose}</p><span class="arrow-label">Explore network →</span>
 </a>)}</div>
 <div class="grid-main" style="margin-top:30px">
  <div class="paper panel"><p class="kicker">A tested protection</p><h3 style="margin:12px 0">The employee PC cannot open the firewall administration page.</h3><p class="muted" style="margin-top:11px">On CLIENT, two security blocks precede a broad allow rule. The hardening validation tested blocked management reachability alongside working DNS, directory, shared-file and internet paths.</p><a class="evidence-link" href={base+"network/zones/client/"}>Follow the CLIENT rules →</a></div>
  <div class="paper panel"><p class="kicker">A deliberate limitation</p><h3 style="margin:12px 0">Having VLANs does not automatically mean least privilege.</h3><p class="muted" style="margin-top:11px">Management, infrastructure, application and DMZ still contain broad pass rules. The dedicated privileged-admin management isolation work remains deferred.</p><a class="evidence-link" href={base+"network/rules/"}>Explore the rule order →</a></div>
 </div>
 <div class="note" style="margin-top:28px"><strong>A critical distinction:</strong> WireGuard remote access is an encrypted tunnel—not a sixth VLAN. The original five VLANs are MGMT, INFRA, APP, CLIENT and DMZ. <a href={base+"reference/explore/NETWORK/"}>Read the documented architecture ↗</a>.</div>
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
